  "worked_solution": "Suppose $I\u2286R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\u2282I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},\u2026,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
